  "body": "# magpie fails to detect the local OpenCode 2 install and still reports OpenCode 1.17.18\n\n## Summary\nmagpie does not detect the OpenCode 2 installed on my machine. It still reports OpenCode 1.17.18, even though running `opencode` in the terminal starts OpenCode 2 (v2.1.4). The cause appears to be that two different `opencode` binaries are installed, and magpie picks the older one.\n\n## Feature request / Goal\nPlease make magpie detect OpenCode 2 correctly, so that I can use magpie to configure the model provider(s) inside OpenCode 2 (the same way it works for other agents). Right now magpie appears to be attached to the old OpenCode 1.x install, so I cannot manage OpenCode 2's model provider from magpie.\n\nConcretely, I would like:\n1. magpie detects the `opencode` binary that my shell actually runs, and shows its correct version (v2.1.4).\n2. magpie can read and write the model provider configuration of OpenCode 2, including any config path or format changes compared to 1.x.\n3. If multiple `opencode` installs exist, magpie shows which one it uses and lets me pick one or set a custom binary path.\n\n## Environment\n- OS: macOS 26\n- magpie version: 0.1.1608\n- Shell: zsh\n- OpenCode in my shell: v2.1.4\n- Two installs: `~/.opencode/bin/opencode` (v2.1.4) and `/opt/homebrew/bin/opencode` (1.17.18)\n\n## Steps to reproduce\n1. Have OpenCode 1.17.18 installed via Homebrew (`/opt/homebrew/bin/opencode`), then install OpenCode 2 to `~/.opencode/bin`.\n2. Open a new terminal and run `opencode --version`. It prints `opencode v2.1.4`.\n3. Open magpie and check the OpenCode entry. <specify the page/section>\n4. magpie shows OpenCode 1.17.18.\n\n## Expected behavior\nmagpie detects the same `opencode` binary that the terminal runs, shows the OpenCode 2 version, and allows configuring the OpenCode 2 model provider.\n\n## Actual behavior\nmagpie shows OpenCode 1.17.18, which matches the Homebrew binary. As a result I cannot configure the OpenCode 2 model provider through magpie.\n\n## Diagnostics\n```\n$ which -a opencode\n~/.opencode/bin/opencode\n/opt/homebrew/bin/opencode\n/opt/homebrew/bin/opencode\n\n$ opencode --version\nopencode v2.1.4\n\n$ ~/.opencode/bin/opencode --version\nopencode v2.1.4\n\n$ /opt/homebrew/bin/opencode --version\n1.17.18\n\n$ echo $PATH\n~/.opencode/bin:~/.local/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/opt/homebrew/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin:/Applications/iTerm.app/Contents/Resources/utilities\n```\n(PATH shortened: duplicate entries and system-internal paths removed. Home directory replaced with `~`.)\n\nIn my interactive shell, `~/.opencode/bin` comes first in PATH, so the terminal runs v2.1.4. The version magpie reports (1.17.18) matches the binary in `/opt/homebrew/bin`.\n\n## Possible cause\nmagpie, as a GUI app, probably does not inherit my interactive shell PATH. It likely falls back to a default PATH or a fixed list of locations where `/opt/homebrew/bin` comes before `~/.opencode/bin`, or it does not check `~/.opencode/bin` at all. I have not confirmed this.\n\n## Questions\n- How does magpie locate the OpenCode binary and read its version?\n- Does magpie search `~/.opencode/bin` (the default location of the official OpenCode install script)?\n- Is there a way to force a re-scan or set a custom binary path?\n- Is OpenCode 2 provider configuration planned or supported?\n",
