     "body": "# OpenCode 2 shows a stale model list: models added in magpie land in the v1 `provider` block, while OpenCode 2 reads `providers`\n\n## Summary\n\nAfter adding a new provider/channel in magpie (call it `<channel>`, serving `<channel>/<model>`), that model never shows up when starting a new conversation in a client that drives OpenCode v2.1.4. `magpie models opencode` says the model is exposed to OpenCode and the gateway serves it, but `opencode models` does not list it.\n\nThe cause looks like a config-format mismatch: `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json` carries the magpie provider **twice** — a v1 `provider.magpie` block (which magpie keeps updating) and a v2 `providers.magpie` block (an older conversion). OpenCode 2 reads `providers`, so anything magpie adds later is invisible to it.\n\n## Environment\n\n- Windows 10 19045\n- magpie 0.1.1714\n- OpenCode v2.1.4 (the build bundled with the client app, OpenChamber)\n- config: `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json`\n- gateway: `http://127.0.0.1:3425/v1`\n\n## Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Have magpie connected to OpenCode, so `provider.magpie` exists with `baseURL: http://127.0.0.1:3425/v1`.\n2. Add a new provider/channel in magpie so a new model appears in its catalog.\n3. Start a new conversation in OpenCode 2 and open the model picker.\n\n## Expected\n\nThe new model is offered — which is what `magpie models opencode` and `magpie visible` claim.\n\n## Actual\n\nThe model is missing. `opencode models` prints 38 models and none of them comes from the new channel.\n\n## Evidence\n\n```\n$ magpie models opencode\n  ...\n  <channel>\n  <channel>/<model>                                <Display Name>  low/high/max\n  opencode is shown every provider's models · magpie visible opencode <family>,… narrows it\n\n$ curl -s http://127.0.0.1:3425/v1/models | jq -r '.data[].id' | wc -l\n43        # includes the newly added <channel>/<model>\n\n$ opencode models | wc -l\n38        # the new model is not among them\n```\n\nIn `~/.config/opencode/opencode.json`:\n\n| block | entries | has the new model |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `provider.magpie.models` (v1) | 43 | yes |\n| `providers.magpie.models` (v2) | 38 | no |\n\nThe 38 models printed by `opencode models` are exactly the 38 in `providers.magpie.models`. Conversely, 9 models exist only in the v1 block — the newly added one among them — and never reach OpenCode 2.\n\nThe v2 block is visibly an older conversion of the v1 one: it still carries `package: \"aisdk:@ai-sdk/openai-compatible\"`, and for an already-known model it keeps an older `name` that the v1 entry no longer has.\n\n## Possible cause\n\nmagpie writes the v1 `provider` schema (OpenCode < 2). OpenCode 2 reads `providers` (`package: \"aisdk:…\"`, `settings.baseURL` / `settings.apiKey`, models with `modelID`, `capabilities`, `variants: [{id, settings}]`, `limit`). Since a v2 block is already present in the file — the client app writes it, it has a v1→v2 converter (`npm` → `aisdk:` package) — OpenCode 2 never picks up what magpie writes afterwards.\n\nI have not confirmed how magpie decides which schema to write (detected OpenCode version? presence of `provider` in the file?). That is possibly the same detection problem as #3828, but the missing piece here is the write side: OpenCode 2's `providers` block is simply never refreshed.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nWhen the OpenCode target is v2, write/refresh `providers.<id>` in the v2 schema — or keep both blocks in sync whenever the exposed model list changes — so a newly added provider shows up in OpenCode 2 without a manual edit. This looks like the \"OpenCode 2 config paths/format\" half that #3828's comment left out of scope.\n\n## Impact\n\nEvery provider or channel added in magpie after the v2 block was created stays invisible in OpenCode 2 / OpenChamber until that block is rebuilt by hand, even though magpie reports the model as exposed.\n",
